60,3,1,talk,Mio,,"Option C is way too risky! If we just split it flat, the people in the cold rooms will just suffer in silence to avoid being the ""expensive"" one. I can't support that.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Option B is technically the most fair, but the installation costs are far too high. We cannot justify spending that much upfront when we are already struggling with payment consistency.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Rin,,"Option A sounds like a great adventure! We just agree on a number and don't have to do that boring, long math every single month. It saves so much time!","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"Option A is just kicking the can down the road. We'll spend the whole year guessing, only to have a massive, stressful argument when the final bill arrives. That's not a solution; it's a delayed explosion.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Misaki,,"Option C is what we have now, and it's already causing the resentment and avoidance I mentioned. If we keep it, we're just choosing to let the current tension continue.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Mio,,"@Shunichi, you're right about the ""delayed explosion""! But @Rin, if we do Option A, we can at least talk about the expected costs now so no one is surprised in winter. What do you think?","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shunichi"",""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Mio, talking now is helpful, but Option A doesn't solve the disparity in usage. It only stabilizes the monthly payment. We still need to address the actual cost distribution without breaking the bank on meters.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Rin,,"@Ryuji, but if we do Option B, who has to read all those meters every month? I saw another house doing that, and it looked like a total drag! I'd rather just have a simple number.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Rin, your aversion to a little effort is charming, but it's not a valid reason to ignore the fact that some of us are paying for others' heaters. Option B is the only one that actually solves the problem.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Shunichi, while Option B is the most precise, we have to consider the cost Ryuji mentioned. If the installation fee is too high, it might just create a new financial stressor for us to argue about.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shunichi"",""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Mio,,"@Misaki, exactly! If we spend all our money on meters, we'll just be stressed about something else. But I really can't vote for C. I want us to be cozy without feeling guilty!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Shunichi, while I agree that fairness is necessary, the cost of Option B is prohibitive. We cannot ignore the immediate financial burden of installation. Option A provides the stability I'm looking for regarding monthly budgeting.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Rin,,"@Shunichi, it's not just about being lazy! If the person in charge of the meters or the spreadsheet gets super stressed, they'll be grumpy with everyone. Option A is way faster and keeps us happy!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Rin, being ""happy"" by ignoring the truth is just a fantasy. @Ryuji, if the installation cost is the only hurdle for Option B, we should discuss a payment plan rather than settling for a system that rewards the heaviest users.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Rin"",""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Misaki,,"@Shunichi, a payment plan is just another debt to manage and another potential point of conflict. We need a system that reduces friction, not one that adds a new financial obligation to our shared living arrangement.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Mio,,"I'm leaning towards Option A! It's not perfect, but it lets us talk about our needs now so no one has to freeze in a coat just to be ""fair."" I'm voting for A!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,"Option B is too expensive, and Option C is already failing us. Option A allows for predictable budgeting and avoids immediate debt, which is the most rational path forward. I vote for A.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Rin,,I'm voting for A too! It's the only one that doesn't turn our home into a math classroom or a construction site. Let's keep it simple and fun! I vote A!,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,I'll be the odd one out here. I refuse to vote for a system that essentially asks the light users to subsidize the heavy ones. I'm voting for B.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Misaki,,"I agree with the majority. While Option B is the most precise, the risk of new financial stress and the administrative burden would only fuel the tension we're trying to escape. I vote for A.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
